diagnosis_text:
Inter-VLAN routing between VLAN 20 (172.16.20.0/24) and VLAN 10 (172.16.10.0/24) is working normally. From the affected client Laptop0 (172.16.20.10), pings to VLAN 10 hosts succeed: 172.16.10.4 (PC4) returned 3/3 replies with RTT ~2.3 ms and TTL 63, and 172.16.10.5 (PC5) also returned 3/3 replies. Reverse direction is also good: PC4 to Laptop0 shows 3/3 replies. This confirms bidirectional L3 connectivity across the router.

On router0, both subinterfaces are present and up: eth0.10 at 172.16.10.1/24 and eth0.20 at 172.16.20.1/24 (ip link/ifconfig show UP, LOWER_UP). The routing table has connected routes for both /24s, ip_forward is enabled (net.ipv4.ip_forward=1), and there are no filtering rules in place (iptables policies are ACCEPT with no denies). This localizes the inter-VLAN gateway to be healthy, with no ACL blocking VLAN 20 → VLAN 10.

Root cause: No current inter-VLAN routing fault was found; the reported outage is not reproducible and was likely transient or endpoint-specific (e.g., brief ARP/neighbor cache issue).

Corrective fix: No changes required. Ask the user to retry. If the issue recurs intermittently, capture ARP tables on Laptop0 and router0 during the event and check for port flaps on switch1 port fa7 (Laptop0) and the trunk to mswitch1, but at present the path is operating correctly.
=== END ===